1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:11,590
Nearly 2,000 years ago, as the Romans began to pull out of Scotland, they left behind a curious treasure: 10 tons of nails, nearly a million of the things.
近2000年前，罗马人开始撤出苏格兰时，留下了一批奇特的宝藏：10吨钉子，将近100万枚。

2
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:18,740
The nail hoard was discovered in 1960 in a four-metre-deep pit covered by two metres of gravel.
1960年，人们在一个4米深的坑里发现了这批窖藏的钉子，坑上覆盖着2米厚的砾石。

3
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:21,860
Why had the Romans buried a million nails?
罗马人为什么要埋下100万枚钉子？

4
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:31,540
The likely explanation is that the withdrawal was rushed, and they didn’t want the local Caledonians getting their hands on 10 tons of weapon-grade iron.
可能的解释是：当时撤退仓促，而且他们不想让当地的喀里多尼亚人弄到10吨可用来制造武器的铁。

5
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:37,680
The Romans buried the nails so deep that they would not be discovered for almost two millennia.
罗马人把钉子埋得如此之深，以至于此后近两千年都无人发现。

6
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:44,580
Later civilisations would value the skilled blacksmith’s labour in a nail even more than the raw material.
后来的文明看重熟练铁匠打制一枚钉子所付出的劳动，甚至胜过看重原材料。

7
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:55,920
As Roma Agrawal explains in her delightful new book Nuts and Bolts, early 17th-century Virginians would sometimes burn down their homes if they were planning to relocate.
正如罗玛·阿格拉沃尔在她妙趣横生的新书《螺母与螺栓》中所解释的，17世纪初的弗吉尼亚人如果打算搬家，有时会把自己的房子烧掉。

8
00:00:56,720 --> 00:01:02,440
This was an attempt to recover the valuable nails, which could be reused after sifting the ashes.
这样做是试图回收那些值钱的钉子：筛过灰烬之后，这些钉子可以重新使用。

9
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:12,790
The idea that one might burn down an entire house just to reclaim the nails underlines how scarce, costly and valuable the simple-seeming technology was.
竟会有人仅仅为了回收钉子就把整座房子烧掉，这一点凸显出这项看似简单的技术当时是多么稀缺、昂贵和珍贵。

10
00:01:13,590 --> 00:01:23,190
The price of nails fell by 90% between the late 1700s and mid-1900s, as economist Daniel Sichel points out in a research paper.
正如经济学家丹尼尔·西切尔在一篇研究论文中指出的，从18世纪末到20世纪中叶，钉子的价格下降了90%。

11
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:37,330
According to Sichel, although the falling price of nails was driven partly by cheaper iron and cheaper energy, most of the credit goes to nail manufacturers who simply found more efficient ways to turn steel into nails.
西切尔认为，钉子价格下降虽然部分是受铁和能源降价的推动，但大部分功劳要归于钉子制造商，他们只不过是找到了把钢制成钉子的更高效方法。

12
00:01:38,130 --> 00:01:43,950
Nails themselves have changed over the years, but Sichel studied them because they haven’t changed much.
钉子本身随着时间的推移确实有所变化，但西切尔之所以研究钉子，是因为它们变化不大。

13
00:01:44,750 --> 00:01:52,290
Roman lamps and Roman chariots are very different from LED strips and sports cars, but Roman nails are still clearly nails.
古罗马的灯和战车与LED灯带和跑车大不相同，但古罗马的钉子显然仍是钉子。

14
00:01:53,090 --> 00:02:01,820
It would be absurd to try to track the changing price of sports cars since 1695, but to ask the same question of nails makes perfect sense.
试图追踪跑车自1695年以来的价格变化会很荒唐，但对钉子提出同样的问题却完全说得通。

15
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:08,180
I make no apology for being obsessed by a particular feature of these objects: their price.
我痴迷于这些物品的一个特定方面——它们的价格，对此我并不觉得有什么可抱歉的。

16
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:10,770
I am an economist, after all.
毕竟，我是个经济学家。

17
00:02:11,570 --> 00:02:23,670
One thing I’ve learnt from writing two books about the history of inventions is that while it is the enchantingly sophisticated technologies that get all the hype, it’s the cheap technologies that change the world.
在写两本发明史著作的过程中，我认识到一点：虽然受到大肆炒作的都是那些精巧得令人着迷的技术，但改变世界的却是廉价技术。

18
00:02:24,470 --> 00:02:39,820
The Gutenberg printing press transformed civilisation not by changing the nature of writing but by changing its cost—and it would have achieved little without a parallel collapse in the price of surfaces to write on, thanks to an often-overlooked technology called paper.
古登堡印刷机彻底改变了文明，靠的不是改变书写的本质，而是改变书写的成本；而且，如果不是书写载体的价格同时暴跌，它也难有多大作为——这次暴跌要归功于一项常被忽视的技术：纸。

19
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:47,050
Solar panels had few niche uses until they became cheap; now they are transforming the global energy system.
在变得便宜之前，太阳能电池板只有寥寥几种小众用途；如今，它们正在彻底改变全球能源体系。
